
:: day_1_1
<<nobr>>
    <<set $day_1_8_lenna = true>>
    <<set $barmaid_done = true>>
    <<set $arm_wrestler_done = true>>
    <<set $day_1_4 = true>>
    <<set $check_card_1 = true>>
    <<set $grent_1 = true>>
    <<set $leave_table_1 = true>>
    <<set $day_1_8_lenna = true>>
<</nobr>>
<img src="img/locations/valdmere/broken_antler_room.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />
I woke to the sound of coins. Small sound. Pathetic sound. The soft clink-clink of copper on copper, like a mouse clearing its throat. Mira was sitting on the edge of the bed   our bed, the one nearest the window, the one we'd stopped pretending wasn't shared about three weeks back   counting what was left of our fortune on her palm. 

Her cropped copper hair caught the gray morning light filtering through the shutters and turned it into something almost pretty. The rest of the room was not pretty. Cracked plaster ceiling. A water stain shaped like a horse that I'd been staring at for five nights running. Walls the color of old teeth. The Broken Antler tavern, Valdmere's finest establishment for people whose life choices had gone spectacularly sideways.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Nine.
<</dialog>>

She said it without turning around. Just that one word, flat as a blade laid on a table.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Nine silver?
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/day_1/1.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

<<dialog "Mira">>
Nine copper. Don't insult me by pretending you thought I meant silver.
<</dialog>>

I sat up. The mattress   more lump than cushion, more regret than comfort   groaned under me like it shared my opinion of the morning. I rubbed my face. Three days of stubble scraped against my palms, rough and familiar, the perpetual scruff I maintained because I was too lazy to shave daily and too vain to let it grow wild. My jaw was thick under my fingers, square-cornered, the heavy bone structure I'd inherited from my father along with his green eyes and his talent for making terrible financial decisions.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Nine coppers is enough for breakfast. Maybe.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Nine coppers is enough for the kind of breakfast that makes you wish you'd skipped it. Stale bread. Hard cheese. Ale that tastes like someone wrung out a dishrag and bottled the runoff.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
So our usual, then.
<</dialog>>

She turned and looked at me. Those amber eyes   pale, luminous, the color that most half-elves carried like a badge of their mixed blood. In dim light they caught whatever illumination was available and threw it back at you, unsettling and beautiful in equal measure. Right now they were catching the gray morning and reflecting something that looked uncomfortably like worry.

Mira didn't worry. Mira planned, assessed, strategized, and occasionally threatened. But she did not worry. The fact that her expression was edging toward it told me more about our situation than the nine coppers did.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Room's paid through today. After that, we're sleeping on the docks.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The docks aren't so bad.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The fish-gutting docks, Aldric. The ones that smell like fish guts. Because they are covered in fish guts. Because that is where the fish are gutted.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Point taken.
<</dialog>>

I swung my legs out of bed. My body moved the way it always moved   heavy, solid, the predictable machinery of a man who'd been swinging a sword since he was sixteen and carrying everything he owned on his back since nineteen. Broad shoulders, thick arms, the working muscle of a sellsword who couldn't afford a training hall and didn't need one because the world was his training hall and it charged admission in blood and bruises. I stood, stretched, and felt my spine crack in three places.

Twenty-six years old. Felt like forty on mornings like this.

Mira pocketed the nine coppers   all of them, which I noted but didn't challenge because she was better with money than I was, which was like saying a fish was better at swimming than a stone   and stood. She was already dressed. She was always already dressed. In three months of sharing beds and roads and everything in between, I'd learned that Mira woke before dawn by instinct, dressed in the dark by habit, and had assessed every threat and opportunity within a hundred paces before I'd finished blinking the sleep from my eyes.

Half-elf metabolism. Leaner, faster, more efficient than human in every measurable way. It was annoying as hell.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We'll figure something out. We always do.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We always figure something out because one of us is a relentless optimist and the other one actually makes plans. Guess which one you are.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The handsome one?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Put your trousers on, handsome. We're going downstairs before the bread gets any staler.
<</dialog>>
<<addobjective "day_1" "Go downstairs for Breakfast">>
<<set $day_1_2 = false>>
[[Go downstairs for Breakfast|broken_antler]]

:: day_1_2
<<set $day_1_2 = true>>
<<changestat "energy"   10>>
Breakfast was exactly as bad as predicted. The common room of the Broken Antler was the architectural equivalent of a sigh   low ceilings stained brown with decades of pipe smoke, furniture that had been repaired so many times it was more nail than wood, a floor that stuck to your boots in ways that demanded you not investigate the cause. The hearth was cold. The windows were grimy. The innkeeper   a heavyset man with the conversational range of a brick   stood behind the bar doing something to his ledger that might have been arithmetic or might have been doodling. Hard to tell. Hard to care.

He set two plates in front of us without being asked. Stale bread, hard cheese, two cups of the house ale. The bread had the consistency of a cobblestone's friendlier cousin. The cheese had achieved the kind of hardness that suggested it had ambitions of becoming furniture. The ale tasted like guilt dissolved in rainwater. I ate. You don't get picky when you're broke.

Mira picked at the bread with her fingers, tearing it into small pieces and eating them one at a time. In her other hand, the arrowhead   one of her broadheads, wicked-looking thing with three razor edges   which she used to pick her teeth between bites. Click. Click. Click. The sound of the metal against her canines set my nerves on edge the same way it had since Thornwall, which was to say, constantly, persistently, and with the grim reliability of sunrise.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Must you?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Must I what?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The teeth thing. With the arrow. It sounds like a tiny blacksmith working on a tiny anvil inside your mouth.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It helps me think.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
About what?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
About how we're going to avoid sleeping next to a pile of fish intestines tomorrow night. Unless you'd prefer I stop thinking about that.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Think away.
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/day_1/2.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Click. Click. Click. I drank the terrible ale and stared at the rain-streaked shutters. Outside, Valdmere was doing what Valdmere always did in autumn   drowning. It had been raining for two days straight, the kind of cold, relentless downpour that found every crack in your boots and every gap in your collar and settled into your bones like a tenant who'd lost the deposit and didn't care. The cobblestones would be rivers. The market stalls would be shuttered. The whole city would be wrapped in a wet gray misery that matched the wet gray misery sitting in my chest.

<<dialog "Mira">>
We have options.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Let's hear them.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Day labor at the docks. They always need muscle to load cargo ships, especially in rain when the regular workers call in sick. Pays about three coppers a day.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Three coppers won't cover the room.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I said we have options. I didn't say they were good options. Second, the mercenary board at the garrison. There might be something posted since last week.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Last week it was rat-clearing and escort jobs that paid in soup.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Third option, we pawn something.
<</dialog>>

That landed on the table between us like a dead bird. We both looked at our weapons   my longsword leaning against the wall, her bow in its oilskin case beside the bed upstairs. The tools of our trade. The only things of value we owned.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
My sword might fetch five silver. If the pawnbroker's feeling generous, which pawnbrokers never are.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
My bow is worth thirty.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You'd sooner cut off your hand.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'd sooner cut off YOUR hand. My hand I need for the bow. Which I'm not pawning. So that option is dead.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Then we're back to dock labor and the garrison board.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We're back to dock labor and the garrison board.
<</dialog>>

She put the arrowhead away. When Mira stopped picking her teeth, it meant she'd finished thinking and had arrived at a conclusion. The conclusion, from the set of her jaw and the flatness of her amber eyes, was not one that made her happy.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Let's try the garrison first. If there's nothing, we haul cargo in the rain and hate ourselves for it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Sounds like a plan.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It sounds like desperation with a schedule. But it's what we've got.
<</dialog>>
<<set $day_1_3 = false>>
<<updateobjective "day_1" "Check the garrison board for mercenary work">>
<<link "Go to the garrison board at east garrison">>
    <<regionmap "valdmere">>
<</link>>

:: day_1_3
<<set $mcLocation = "east_garrison">>
<<set $day_1_3 = true>>
The east garrison mercenary board was a slab of weathered oak nailed to the wall beside the guardhouse, protected from the rain by an overhang that didn't quite do its job. Half the postings were water-damaged, the ink bleeding into illegibility. The other half were legible and depressing.

I stood in front of it, rain dripping off the brim of my hood, and read each one with the desperate optimism of a man looking for a diamond in a pile of horse manure.

<img src="img/scene/day_1/3.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Rat-clearing. Three coppers, bring your own poison. Same posting from last week.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Next.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Escort contract   merchant family heading north. Payment in meals and lodging, no coin.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We can't eat lodging. Next.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Bounty on a pickpocket. Two silver. Been posted for three weeks. Either the pickpocket is very good or two silver isn't worth the effort.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Both. Next.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That's it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
That can't be it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
There's one more, but it's asking for a wizard and I checked this morning   I'm still not a wizard.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Have you tried?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Tried what? Being a wizard? You don't just try being a wizard, Mira. It requires years of training and a fundamental connection to the arcane that 
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I meant have you tried reading the fine print. Sometimes wizard-required postings accept armed escort as a substitute if the client is desperate enough.
<</dialog>>

I leaned closer. Squinted through the rain at the smudged text. The client wanted a wizard to clear a haunted cellar. Specifically, the ghost of a cat that was scaring the household staff. Payment, one silver and a basket of turnips.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
It's a ghost cat. For turnips.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I hate this city.
<</dialog>>

We left the garrison. The rain was still falling. Valdmere was between conflicts   the border dispute with the northern provinces had settled, the pirate activity off the coast had been quelled by a naval expedition last month, and the internal guild wars had reached a temporary truce that left everyone armed and unemployed. The mercenary market was flooded with idle swords, and the pay had cratered to the point where honest labor at the docks was starting to look competitive.

We walked through the market district because it was on the way back and because sometimes walking past things you can't afford is its own kind of entertainment. The stalls were half-shuttered against the rain, their awnings sagging with pooled water, the merchants huddled under them like refugees from joy.

Mira stopped at a fletcher's stall. The fletcher   a lean, sun-browned woman with arms like braided rope   had her wares displayed under a waxed canvas tent, arrows, bowstrings, quivers, a pair of hand-carved crossbow bolts that were more art than weapon.

<img src="img/scene/day_1/4.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

<<dialog "Mira">>
Look at that bowstring.
<</dialog>>

She said it the way another woman might say "look at that necklace" or "look at that dress." With longing. With the hungry reverence of someone who appreciated the craft behind the object. The bowstring was silk-and-sinew blend, treated with something that gave it a faint luster even in the gray light. Professional grade. The kind of string that turned a good bow into a great one.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
How much?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
More than nine coppers. Don't ask.
<</dialog>>

She walked away. Didn't look back. I filed it away   the way she'd looked at the bowstring, the particular angle of her chin when she'd forced herself to move on. If we ever had money again, I'd come back and buy it for her. I wouldn't tell her I was doing it. She'd punch me for being sentimental and then she'd string her bow with it and never mention it again, and that would be her way of saying thank you.

Two stalls down, a blacksmith was repairing a sword with a technique I'd never seen   folding the heated metal at an angle that shouldn't have worked but somehow did, the hammer strikes producing a tone like a bell rather than the usual dull ring. I stopped to watch. The rhythm was hypnotic. Strike, fold, strike, turn, strike, fold. The metal glowed cherry-red under the rain, steam rising from the anvil in thin white ghosts.

<img src="img/scene/day_1/5.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Ten minutes passed. The blade took shape   a short sword, curved slightly, with a single edge and a fuller groove that would make it whistle when swung. Beautiful work. The kind of craft that separated a smith who made swords from a smith who made weapons.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Are you going to stand here all day watching a man hit metal?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
He's not just hitting metal. Look at the fold angle   that's a northern technique. You don't see that this far south. The grain structure will be 
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Aldric.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We are broke. We are homeless as of tomorrow. I love that you appreciate craftsmanship. I do. It's charming. But if I stand in this rain for one more minute watching you watch a blacksmith, I am going to take that glowing sword and insert it somewhere creative.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Fine. We're going.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We're going.
<</dialog>>

We went. But I looked back once. The smith caught my eye and gave me a nod   one craftsman to another, even though my craft was using the blade rather than making it. I nodded back. Nice moment. Didn't pay the rent.

On the way back, Mira suggested we spend two coppers on decent ale from a proper taproom rather than the Broken Antler's liquid punishment.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We could. Two coppers for something that actually tastes like ale instead of like regret.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Or we save the two coppers and drink regret at the Antler for free. Since we're already paying for the room.
<</dialog>>

We looked at each other. The rain dripped off our hoods. A cart rolled past, splashing mud across both our boots. Somewhere nearby, a dog barked at nothing.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Save it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Save it.
<</dialog>>

We walked back to the Broken Antler in silence. Sometimes saving two coppers feels like winning. Most of the time it just feels like being poor.
<<nobr>>
    <<set $barmaid_done = false>>
    <<set $arm_wrestler_done = false>>
    <<set $day_1_4 = false>>
    <<set $board_sergeant_start = false>>
    <<addsidequest "barmaids_eye" "The Barmaid's Eye" "Talk with Her">>
    <<addsidequest "arm_wrestler" "The Arm Wrestler" "Challenge Him">>
<</nobr>>
<<updateobjective "day_1" "Go back to the Broken Antler">>
<<link "Walk back to the Broken Antler">>
    <<regionmap "valdmere">>
<</link>>

:: day_1_4
<<set $mcLocation = "broken_antler">>
<<set $day_1_4 = true>>
The common room was emptier in the afternoon. A few day-drinkers nursing their tankards like the ale might run away if they let go. The old man in the corner   the one who'd been there every single day since we arrived, asleep with a fat orange cat on his chest, both of them breathing in synchronized wheezes   was still there. Still asleep. The cat opened one eye when we walked in, judged us, and closed it again.

I ordered two of the terrible house ales. Three coppers each. Six total. That left us three coppers to our name. Three coppers between two armed professionals who'd been traveling together for three months and had somehow managed to spend every coin they earned faster than they earned it.

My father would have been proud. He'd been a master of the same trick, back before the lord took the farm and the drink took him.

Mira settled into our corner booth   the one with the high-backed seats that let us keep our backs to the wall and our eyes on the door. Old habit. Professional paranoia. She arranged herself the way she always did, bow case under the table within reach, short sword at her hip, a clear line of sight to both exits.

<<dialog "Mira">>
We need to think differently.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Differently how?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The garrison board is dead. The docks pay pennies. We're not pawning weapons. So we need a different channel. Private contracts. Under-the-table work. The kind of jobs that don't get posted on public boards because the people offering them don't want public attention.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You're talking about the kind of work that gets you stabbed in an alley.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm talking about the kind of work that pays in silver instead of turnips. There's a difference. Slim difference, admittedly. But present.
<</dialog>>

I drank my ale. Tasted like defeat with a frothy head. The common room was dim, the afternoon light barely making it through the grimy windows. Rain hammered the shutters in irregular bursts, each one a reminder that the world outside was wet and cold and not interested in our problems. That's when I noticed the board.

Not the garrison board   a different one. Smaller, nailed to the wall near the bar, half-hidden behind a hanging lamp that hadn't been lit. I'd walked past it every day for five days and never looked closely. Tavern-specific postings. Local noise. Lost items, rooms for let, a notice about a missing tabby cat that was probably living with the old man in the corner based on the description.

But one card stood out. Hand-lettered on thick parchment, the kind of paper that cost money, which meant whoever wrote it had some. The ink was dark, the letters precise.
<<set $check_card_1 = false>>
<<set $day_1_4 = true>>
<<updateobjective "day_1" "Check the card on the tavern board">>
[[Check the Card|broken_antler]]

:: check_card_1
<<set $check_card_1 = true>>
<span style="color: red;">Discrete procurement services   inquire at bar.</span>

<img src="img/scene/day_1/6.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

I stared at it. "Discrete procurement services" could mean anything from fetching a noble's lost jewelry to smuggling contraband through the harbor gates. The vagueness was deliberate. The kind of posting that filtered out people who needed too many details before committing.

I flagged down the innkeeper. He looked up from his ledger with the enthusiasm of a man being asked to do anything at all.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That card on the board. The procurement services. Who posted it?
<</dialog>>

The innkeeper shrugged   a minimal movement involving exactly one shoulder and zero additional effort.

<<dialog "Innkeeper">>
Man comes in most evenings. Big fellow. Scarred up. Sits at the back, waits for people to ask about the card. Been doing it for about a week.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What kind of work?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Innkeeper">>
The kind I don't ask about. That's the kind of tavern this is.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What time does he usually come in?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Innkeeper">>
Sunset. Give or take. Orders whiskey, sits, waits. Sometimes people talk to him. Sometimes they don't. Either way, he drinks his whiskey and leaves.
<</dialog>>

He went back to his ledger. Conversation over. I returned to the booth and told Mira.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Discrete procurement services. That's a smuggling job.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Or a recovery job. Or a delivery job. Or an assassination, I suppose, if we're being thorough about the possibilities.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We don't do assassinations.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I know we don't do assassinations. I'm listing options.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Well, stop listing that one. What time did the innkeeper say?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Sunset. We wait?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We wait.
<</dialog>>

She picked up her arrowhead. Click. Click. Click. Thinking again. I drank my ale and watched the rain and tried to calculate how many hours of terrible ale I could stretch three coppers across. The answer was, not enough.
<<updateobjective "day_1" "Wait for sunset">>
<<link "Wait for sunset">>
    <<goto "day_1_5">>
<</link>>

:: day_1_5
Sunset in Valdmere was less an event and more a negotiation. The sun didn't so much set as give up, the gray sky darkening by degrees until the lamplighters came out with their poles and the city shifted from daytime misery to nighttime misery with marginally better lighting.

The Broken Antler's evening crowd filtered in. Dock workers with calloused hands and hollow eyes, looking for the particular kind of oblivion that three-copper ale provided. A pair of off-duty city guards sharing a pitcher and trading complaints about their sergeant. A group of merchants' apprentices   young, loud, soft-handed   occupying a table near the hearth and laughing at things that weren't funny. The fire popped and crackled, throwing warm shadows across the low ceiling and making the common room almost bearable if you squinted hard enough and drank fast enough.

Mira had switched to water an hour ago. I was on my second ale and beginning to regret spending the copper. The common room smelled like pipe smoke, wet wool, and the particular funk of twenty human bodies crammed into a space designed for ten. The cat on the old man's chest had relocated to the bar, where it sat washing itself with the serene indifference of a creature that had never worried about money in its life.

The door opened. The sound of the storm rushed in   wind, rain, the distant clatter of cart wheels on wet cobblestones   and with it came a man who matched the innkeeper's description the way a landslide matches the word "inconvenient." Wide as a wardrobe. Face that was mostly scar tissue, the kind of face that suggested he'd lost a fight with a cheese grater and won the rematch by eating it. Heavy jaw. Small eyes that moved fast and missed nothing. An oilskin cloak that pooled water on the floor as he crossed the threshold.

<img src="img/scene/day_1/7.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Mira's elbow found my ribs. She didn't look at me. Didn't need to.

<<dialog "Mira">>
That's him.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You think?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Big. Scarred. Oilskin. Ordering whiskey. Either that's our man or the innkeeper's description applies to half the muscle in Valdmere.
<</dialog>>

The man   Grent, presumably   collected his whiskey from the bar, exchanged exactly zero words with the innkeeper, and moved to a table at the back of the room. He sat with his shoulders to the wall and his eyes on the door. The same instinct Mira and I had. Professional paranoia recognizes its own kind.

He didn't look at us. Didn't look at anyone. He drank his whiskey with the patient stillness of a man who'd done this every evening for a week and would do it every evening for a week more if necessary. A man who understood that the right kind of work found the right kind of people, and the right kind of people were the ones desperate enough to approach a stranger in a tavern based on a card with three vague words on it.

I looked at Mira. She looked at our three coppers.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Well? You going to sit here until we can't afford to sit here anymore?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I was hoping you'd go. You're more intimidating.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm an archer. You're the one with the sword and the jaw. Go sell us.
<</dialog>>
<<set $grent_1 = false>>
<<updateobjective "day_1" "Approch Grent about the card">>
[[Approch Grent about the card|broken_antler]]

:: grent_1
<<set $grent_1 = true>>
I picked up my ale   mostly for something to do with my hands   and crossed the common room. The man watched me approach. Not with alarm, not with welcome. With the flat, evaluating attention of someone who assessed threats and opportunities through the same lens.

I sat down across from him without waiting for an invitation. If he wanted people to approach him about that card, he could tolerate the lack of ceremony.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Saw your card on the board. Discrete procurement services. I'm here to ask about it.
<</dialog>>

He looked at me. Then past me, at Mira in our corner booth   the bow case visible under the table, the short sword at her hip, the particular coiled readiness of someone who could kill you from fifty paces and was mildly annoyed about being alive.

<<dialog "Grent">>
Name's Grent. Merchant Consortium, Valdmere chapter. You two sellswords?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm a sellsword. She's an archer. She'll tell you there's a difference.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Grent">>
Same thing with extra steps. Bring her over.
<</dialog>>

I waved Mira across. She came   unhurried, ale in hand, settling into the chair beside me with the deliberate ease of someone who wanted you to know she was armed and comfortable and not particularly impressed by you. Grent watched her sit with the same flat appraisal he'd given me. Then he reached inside his oilskin cloak, produced a folded parchment, and placed it on the table between us with the precise, deliberate placement of a man laying down a card he knew would change the game.

<<dialog "Grent">>
Got a contract. Night work. Twenty silver each, half up front.
<</dialog>>

Twenty silver. The number hit me like a physical force. Twenty silver was a month of rooms and meals. It was new boots and a proper sharpening stone and ale that didn't taste like someone's worst memory. It was the difference between sleeping on a bed and sleeping on a pile of fish guts. My hand was reaching for the parchment before my brain finished processing, the instinct of a man who'd been broke long enough that money had its own gravitational pull.

Under the table, Mira kicked me. Hard. Right in the shin. The universal signal for don't look desperate, you idiot. I pulled my hand back. But I could see the number working behind her eyes too   those amber half-elf eyes doing the same math mine were doing, calculating room costs and food budgets and how far twenty silver could stretch if you spent it carefully.

Neither of us spent anything carefully. But it was nice to imagine.

<<dialog "Mira">>
What kind of night work?
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/day_1/8.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Grent leaned back. The chair creaked under him like a ship in heavy seas.

<<dialog "Grent">>
Supply caravan got hit three days ago on the Ashenmoor Road. Four leagues east, give or take. Whole wagon of alchemical reagents   sealed crates, specialty goods. Valuable stuff. The guards were killed.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
By what?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Grent">>
Goblins. Warband operating out of the old Greymire mine. Scouts put them at fifteen, maybe twenty strong. Been hitting shipments for weeks   grab and run, nothing organized. The Consortium wants the cargo recovered. Whatever's still intact.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Fifteen to twenty goblins. In a mine.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Grent">>
Small goblins. Big mine.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
There's no such thing as a small goblin when you're underground with fifteen of them. They fight dirty, they know the terrain, and they have the kind of morale that comes from being too stupid to be afraid.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Grent">>
Which is why I'm paying twenty silver each and not five. The Consortium is aware that this job carries risk. We're also aware that the cargo sitting in that mine is worth considerably more than forty silver, which means your compensation is generous but not unreasonable. Think of it as a fair exchange   your skills and your risk tolerance for our money and our problem.
<</dialog>>

Mira's arrowhead came out. She held it between two fingers, turning it slowly, the broadhead catching the firelight. Click against her thumbnail. Click. Click. Her thinking tell. Grent watched the arrowhead with the careful attention of a man who recognized a weapon when he saw one, even a small one being used for dental hygiene.

<<dialog "Mira">>
What's in the crates?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Grent">>
Sealed alchemical reagents. Specialty compounds for clients who pay premium rates and prefer discretion. The specifics are above your pay grade and below your interest, unless you're planning to open an apothecary.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm planning to not get killed by something that leaks out of a broken crate while I'm carrying it through a goblin-infested tunnel in the dark. What kind of reagents? Volatile? Toxic? Corrosive?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Grent">>
Some of all three. Which is why the sealed crates matter and the broken ones don't. If a seal's intact, the contents are stable. If a seal's broken, leave it. Don't open them. Don't drink them. Don't sniff them. Some of that stuff is meant for controlled application by trained alchemists, and the results of uncontrolled exposure range from unpleasant to fatal, with several creative stops in between.
<</dialog>>

He shrugged   a gesture that involved so much shoulder it was practically a geological event. The oilskin cloak rustled like a tent in a gale.

<<dialog "Grent">>
Twenty silver each guaranteed. Plus a two-silver bonus per sealed crate you bring back. The Consortium doesn't expect all of it. Just whatever you can carry.
<</dialog>>

I looked at Mira. She looked at my nine coppers sitting on the table next to the ale tankards, a tiny monument to our failure as functional adults. She looked at the folded parchment with the Consortium seal. She looked at me.

The conversation happened in the space between her blinks.

Her: This is dangerous.

Me: Everything we do is dangerous.

Her: Underground. Close quarters. My bow is useless in a tunnel.

Me: You have the short sword.

Her: I hate that sword.

Me: Twenty silver.

Her: ... Twenty silver.

She put the arrowhead away.

<<dialog "Mira">>
When?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Grent">>
Tonight, if you're smart. Tomorrow at the latest. The longer those crates sit in a goblin nest, the less likely anything inside them survives. Goblins are curious creatures. They poke things. They taste things. Eventually they break things. The window for recovery narrows every hour.
<</dialog>>

I picked up the parchment. The Consortium seal was pressed into the wax   a pair of scales over a ship's wheel, the universal symbol of people who had money and wanted more of it. The contract was straightforward. Recover sealed cargo from the Greymire mine. Return to Consortium warehouse on Dockside Row. Ask for a woman named Voss. She'd handle payment.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We'll go tonight.
<</dialog>>

Under the table, Mira kicked me again. Harder this time. I was going to have a bruise the shape of her boot heel on my shin for a week.

