He produced a small leather pouch from inside his oilskin cloak and counted out twenty silver coins on the table. The sound they made   that particular music, that bright metallic chime of real money hitting wood   was the most beautiful noise I'd heard in weeks. Maybe months. Each coin caught the firelight as it landed, winking like a tiny promise.

Grent divided them into two piles. Ten and ten. He pushed one toward me, one toward Mira. I picked mine up and the weight of them   warm from his pocket, solid, real   settled against my palm like an answer to a prayer I'd been too proud to say.

<<dialog "Grent">>
You get the rest plus bonuses when you deliver the crates. Dockside Row. Voss. She's thorough, so have the cargo count ready.
<</dialog>>

He stood. Reached into his cloak and pulled out a thick charcoal pencil and the napkin from under his whiskey glass. He spread the napkin flat and began sketching   quick, sure lines, the practiced hand of a man who'd drawn tactical maps on worse surfaces.

<<dialog "Grent">>
Upper tunnels branch off the main shaft about fifty paces in. The camp is here   central chamber, old crew quarters. This is where they'll be sleeping. The storage shafts angle down from a junction point here.
<</dialog>>

He jabbed a thick finger at the napkin. The map was crude but clear   entrance, main shaft, junction, the branching paths of upper and lower tunnels.

<<dialog "Grent">>
That's where your crates should be. Heavy things go down. Sleeping things go up. Food goes near the fire. Goblins are simple creatures with simple organizational systems.
