He pulled his oilskin hood back up and headed for the door. At the threshold, the storm reached in and grabbed him   wind whipping his cloak, rain lashing the floor   and he stepped into it without hesitation, the way a man steps into water he's already accepted will be cold. The door closed. The noise died. The common room settled back into its usual background hum of quiet desperation and bad ale.

Mira picked up her ten silver and weighed them in her palm. Her fingers closed around them, the archer's grip, and I watched the tension in her hand   the way she held the coins like they might escape if she loosened her grip even slightly.

