She disappeared up the stairs. The sound of her boots faded. The common room resumed its gentle background noise   murmured conversations, the pop of the fire, the old man's synchronized snoring with his cat.

I sat there for a moment. Ten silver in my belt. A contract in my pocket. A job that might kill me in a few hours and the warm, complicated feeling in my chest that I got every time Mira walked away from me, which was the feeling of watching the person you trusted most in the world put their back to you because they trusted you not to let anything get to it.

I pulled out my longsword and laid it across the table.
