:: day_1_6

The blade was good steel   nothing fancy, no enchantments, no master smith's mark. Just honest iron folded and hammered by a smith in Thornwall who'd learned his trade from his father who'd learned it from his. The kind of sword that working sellswords carried   reliable, balanced, responsive to a trained hand, and completely unremarkable. A blade that wouldn't start a conversation at a noble's table but would end one in an alley.

The edge had nicks from the last job   the rat job, in Thornwall, which I was never going to mention again for the rest of my natural life. Six days clearing a tanner's basement of dire rats, each one the size of a terrier with teeth like broken glass. Five coppers each for the work. Six coppers for the healer afterward. Net loss, one copper and whatever remained of my dignity, which wasn't much.

I drew my whetstone from my belt pouch and began working the edge. Long, steady strokes. The sound of steel on stone filled the corner booth like a meditation. Shhhhk. Shhhhk. Shhhhk. The rhythm was automatic   muscle memory from a thousand evenings just like this one, sitting in a hundred different taverns, sharpening a blade for a hundred different jobs. The whetstone was almost as old as the sword. Flat on one side, slightly concave on the other from years of use. The stone had shaped the blade. The blade had shaped the stone. Together, they'd shaped me.

<img src="img/scene/day_1/10.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

I worked the edge methodically. Base to tip, maintaining the angle, feeling for the burr that told me the metal was thin enough. Turn the blade. Other side. Base to tip. The nicks from the rat job smoothed out under the stone's patient attention. The edge emerged   not razor-sharp, that was for daggers and kitchen knives   but keen. Working-sharp. The kind of edge that would bite into leather, part mail, and find the soft spaces between a goblin's ribs without hesitation.

When I was done, I set my longsword aside and picked up Mira's short sword. The cheese-smelling one.

She was right about the grip. It did smell like old cheese. Not aggressively, not the kind of smell that announced itself from across the room, but a persistent, low-grade funkiness that became apparent the moment you wrapped your hand around the leather binding. I'd asked her once where she'd gotten it. She'd said she took it off a dead bandit outside Kestwick who'd apparently used it to cut cheese with more enthusiasm than hygiene. The blade itself was decent steel   single-edged, slightly curved, good for close-quarters work. The balance was, as she perpetually complained, wrong   slightly blade-heavy, which made it want to chop rather than thrust.

But the edge was good, and it took the whetstone well. I worked it from base to tip, the shorter blade requiring a different stroke rhythm than my longsword. Shhhhk. Shhhhk. By the time I was done, the cheese sword could split a hair lengthwise. She'd still complain about it. She always did.

I oiled both blades with the rag from my belt kit. Drew my fingers along the flat of each one, checking for burrs I might have missed. Clean. Sharp. Ready.

I set the swords aside and looked at the common room.
