:: day_1_7_lenna

The evening crowd had thickened while I worked. The noise level had risen from murmur to roar   the particular volume of a tavern hitting its stride, when the ale was flowing and the conversations were getting louder and the hearth fire was roaring and the whole room felt like a living thing, breathing and pulsing with the collective warmth of thirty people who'd come here to forget, briefly, that their lives were hard.

Dock workers with hands like leather and eyes like stones, drinking fast, talking about the ships. City guards off duty, armor stacked under the table, laughing about something their captain had said. The merchants' apprentices were getting drunker and louder, their laughter high and sharp, the laughter of people who'd never been hungry and didn't know what it felt like. The old man and his cat slept through all of it, undisturbed, a monument to the power of not giving a damn and there was a woman at the bar.

I noticed her the way you notice anyone   peripherally at first, a shape in the crowd, and then with increasing detail as the eye found reasons to return. She was leaning against the bar with one elbow, a tankard in her other hand, watching the room with a lazy, appraising gaze. The look of someone who was exactly where she wanted to be and in no hurry to be anywhere else.

Chestnut hair, loose around her shoulders, catching the firelight in auburn streaks. Brown eyes with gold flecks that reminded me of creek water in autumn. A scatter of freckles across her nose and cheeks, so many I'd need an hour and better light to count them. A bodice laced up the front   laced loosely, deliberately loosely, the kind of looseness that was a statement rather than an oversight. The swell of her breasts visible in the gap, soft and warm-looking, the skin there paler than her freckled face. Full hips under a skirt hemmed high enough to show her calves   strong calves, working calves, the kind that came from standing and walking and living in a body that did things. A soft belly visible in the way the fabric hung. She was built the way real women were built   no sharp angles, no aristocratic thinness, just curves and warmth and the comfortable solidity of a person who used her body every day and took care of it without being precious about it.

<img src="img/scene/day_1/11.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Not stunning. Not the kind of beauty that stopped conversations and started wars. But warm. Real. Available and in that moment   sitting alone in a tavern with a mine job in two hours and the uncertainty of whether I'd see tomorrow   warm and real and available was worth more than stunning could ever be.

She caught me looking. Of course she did. A working girl's eyes were her best tool   she'd spotted me watching before I'd finished deciding to watch. She smiled. A professional smile, warm and inviting, with just enough heat behind the friendliness to communicate the transaction being offered. Not crude. Not aggressive. Just, I see you seeing me. Here's what I can do about it.

She picked up her tankard and walked over. Her walk was unhurried, confident, her hips swaying with the natural rhythm of a woman who was comfortable in her own skin and wanted you to notice that comfort.
