Two silver. I had ten from Grent's advance. I could afford it. The question was whether I should   practical math, cold-blooded arithmetic, the kind of calculation that sellswords made every day. Every coin mattered. We had a job in two hours. Spending money on pleasure before a job was irresponsible, impractical, and the kind of decision my father would have made right before drinking the rest.

But under the practical math, something else was pulling. Something that didn't deal in coppers and silver but in the raw, uncomfortable calculus of mortality. I was twenty-six. Healthy. About to walk into a dark hole full of things that wanted to kill me. The math of mercenary life was simple and brutal, you might not come back. Every night might be the last one. Every ale might be the final taste. Every touch might be the last warmth your skin ever felt and here was Lenna. Warm, willing, gold-flecked eyes, freckles like a constellation chart. Offering me something that had nothing to do with swords or survival.

I opened my mouth to answer and heard footsteps on the stairs behind me. Mira.
